WEBVTT

00:00:00.039 --> 00:00:04.907
This example is designed to show how a small change in a condition changes the result.

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:11.172
First, run the query exactly as shown and inspect which active patients meet the age requirement.

00:00:11.172 --> 00:00:14.007
Then change the age threshold from fifty to sixty and run it again.

00:00:14.007 --> 00:00:19.052
Before the second run, predict which rows should disappear.

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:21.592
Notice that nothing is being updated in the patients table.

00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:26.892
We are only changing the predicate used to decide which rows appear in the SELECT result.

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:31.367
This is one reason SELECT is a safe place to practice logic.

00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:34.847
You can adjust conditions and observe the effect without changing stored data.

00:00:34.847 --> 00:00:39.172
Try the same two versions in pgAdmin if you want additional practice comparing query results.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:44.327
Connect this idea to an application safely inserting or updating business records.

00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:49.102
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:49.102 --> 00:00:55.502
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:55.502 --> 00:01:00.512
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:05.243
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
